use std
into bake.amni

Imports: os, json, gfx, gfx.gltf, gfx.codec_png and gfx.codec_jpeg_enc. This file is a module with no main. It bakes the in-memory state of a loaded glTF asset (moved vertices, repainted textures, changed material factors, hidden meshes) into a new .glb by copying the source file and replacing only what changed, so skins, morph targets, animations and extensions such as VRM stay exactly as they were. Callers mark what changed in the module vars below; `bake_mark` can instead record a checksum of every image right after loading so that `bake_glb` finds repainted images by itself.

Module vars: `var bk_changed int list, empty` (per global image index: 1 when its pixels changed), `var bk_untiled bool list, empty` (per global image index: true when the image was widened to cover a tiled texture, so its material needs a texture transform), `var bk_shape = false` (vertex positions, normals or triangles changed), `var bk_mat = false` (material factors changed) and `var bk_sum u64 list, empty` (per global image index: the checksum of its pixels when `bake_mark` ran, 0 for none).

`emit(d, n, out)` writes node n of the parsed JSON document d (passed by reference) back into the growable byte list out as compact JSON text using the json module's formatters (`json::format_key`, `json::format_string`, `json::format_int`, `json::format_number`, `json::format_bool`, `json::format_null`): objects with their members in order, arrays with their elements, strings, whole numbers as ints, other numbers as numbers.

`bk_hash(b)` returns the 64-bit FNV-1a hash (an unsigned 64-bit number) of the byte slice b.

`bake_mark(asset int)` sizes `bk_sum` to `gltf::images.len` filled with 0, then for each image k of the asset (global index g is `gltf::assets[asset].images.first` plus k) calls `gltf::need_pixels(g)` and sets that entry to `bk_hash(gltf::image_pixels(g))`.

`bake_glb(asset, src, out, prefix)` (the asset index and three paths given as byte slices; it returns true when it wrote the file) writes a copy of the source .glb with what changed. An image of the asset counts as changed when `bk_changed` has a nonzero entry for its global index, or when `bk_sum` has a nonzero entry for it that differs from `bk_hash` of its current pixels (call `gltf::need_pixels` first). Read src with `read_file`; the JSON chunk is at byte 20 with length `u32le(12)`, the binary chunk follows (its length at the 4 bytes after the JSON chunk, its data 8 bytes later). Parse the JSON with `json::parse`. The changed images' old bytes are dropped: call a JSON bufferView dead when only changed images reference it (no accessor and no unchanged image uses it). New binary: for each JSON bufferView in index order, when it is dead it becomes `{"buffer":0,"byteOffset":0,"byteLength":4}` and copies nothing; otherwise copy its bytes (its byteOffset, 0 when absent, and byteLength into the old binary) to the end of the new binary padded with zeros to a multiple of 4, and record its new byteOffset (the other members of the view stay as they were). The new binary starts with 4 zero bytes so dead views have something to point at. Then for each image k of the asset (global image `gltf::assets[asset].images.first + k`) that counts as changed, its new bytes padded with zeros to a multiple of 4, remembering offset and length: when the source image's mimeType is "image/jpeg", the JPEG made with `codec_jpeg_enc::encode(gltf::image_pixels(img), width, height, 92, bytes)` and mime "image/jpeg"; otherwise, when prefix is not empty and the file `prefix + "_tex" + global index + ".png"` can be read, its bytes, else the PNG made with `codec_png::encode(width, height, 4, gltf::image_pixels(img), bytes)`, and mime "image/png". New JSON: emit the root object member by member (walk an object's members with `json::each` over node n, giving each member node c, naming each with `json::name_of(d, c)`, or look one up with `json::member(d, n, key)`; `json::element` only indexes arrays and gives -1 on an object, so never use it for members; the same holds inside materials); for "bufferViews" emit the old elements with their new byteOffset (dead ones as described) and then one `{"buffer":0,"byteOffset":o,"byteLength":l}` per new image; for "images" emit each element as is except changed ones, which become `{"bufferView":v,"mimeType":<its new mime>,"name":<old name or "">}` with v the new view's index; when `bk_shape`, also overwrite vertex data in the new binary: for each mesh k of the JSON "meshes" array (the asset's mesh `gltf::assets[asset].meshes.first + k`) and each of its primitives j (`gltf::prims[mesh.prims.first + j]`), take the accessor `attributes.POSITION`, its bufferView's byteOffset (0 when absent) plus the accessor's byteOffset (0 when absent), the stride (the view's byteStride, else 12) and its count, and write each vertex v's three position floats from `gltf::verts[(prim.vfirst + v) * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS]` as little-endian f32 at that offset plus v times the stride (only when the count equals `prim.vcount`; otherwise see the rebuilt primitives below and skip it here); also set that accessor's "min" and "max" to the new bounds when emitting "accessors". Write `attributes.NORMAL` the same way (stride default 12) from the three floats at `gltf::AT_NRM` of each vertex. When the primitive has an "indices" accessor whose count is at least `prim.icount`, rewrite it at its offset (view byteOffset plus accessor byteOffset) in its componentType (5121 one byte, 5123 two bytes, 5125 four bytes, little-endian): entry t below `prim.icount` is `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst + t]`, every later entry up to its count is 0 (degenerate triangles). For "nodes" emit each node k (the asset's node `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first + k`) as is, except that when the JSON node has a "mesh" member and that node's in-memory `mesh` is negative (hidden), its "mesh" and "skin" members are left out. for "materials" emit each material k (when its base texture's image has a true `bk_untiled` entry, write `extensions.KHR_texture_transform` = `{"scale":[sx,sy]}` with sx and sy the first and fourth numbers of the material's current base texture transform `m`, inside its `pbrMetallicRoughness.baseColorTexture`, replacing any present; this applies even when `bk_mat` is false) (the asset's material `gltf::assets[asset].materials.first + k`) as is except that, when `bk_mat`, its `pbrMetallicRoughness` members `baseColorFactor` (four numbers), `metallicFactor` and `roughnessFactor` are written as the material's current `base`, `metallic` and `roughness`, its `emissiveFactor` as the current `emissive` (three numbers) and, when it has a `normalTexture`, that object's `scale` as its current `normal_scale` and, when it has `extensions.KHR_materials_sheen`, that object's `sheenColorFactor` as its current `sheen` (three numbers) (emit the objects member by member, replacing just those numbers or adding them when absent); for "buffers" emit `[{"byteLength":<new binary length>}]`; everything else as is. Pad the JSON with spaces to a multiple of 4. Write "glTF", version 2, total length, the JSON chunk (length, "JSON") and the binary chunk (length, "BIN" and a zero byte), all little-endian, with `write_file`. Print "glb " plus the path and return true; return false when the source cannot be read or parsed. A primitive whose POSITION count differs from `prim.vcount` is rebuilt (when `bk_shape`) if it has no "targets" member, else it keeps its original data and prints "bake: mesh " plus the mesh name (`gltf::text_of(mesh.name)`) plus " changed its vertex count, kept the original". Rebuilding appends new data after the image views: for each attribute the JSON primitive lists, among POSITION (three floats at `gltf::AT_POS`), NORMAL (three at `gltf::AT_NRM`), TANGENT (four at `gltf::AT_TAN`), TEXCOORD_0 (two at `gltf::AT_UV0`), TEXCOORD_1 (two at `gltf::AT_UV1`), COLOR_0 (four at `gltf::AT_COL`), JOINTS_0 (four at `gltf::AT_JOINT`, stored as unsigned 16-bit integers, componentType 5123) and WEIGHTS_0 (four at `gltf::AT_WEIGHT`), the values of the vertices from `prim.vfirst` for `prim.vcount` vertices as one new bufferView (padded to a multiple of 4) and one new accessor (componentType 5126 except for joints, type "VEC2", "VEC3" or "VEC4" by the number of values, count `prim.vcount`, and for POSITION also "min" and "max"); and the `prim.icount` entries of `gltf::indices` from `prim.ifirst` as unsigned 32-bit integers (componentType 5125, type "SCALAR") in one more view and accessor. The new accessors are emitted after the old ones in "accessors" and the new views after the image views in "bufferViews"; when emitting "meshes", a rebuilt primitive's attributes point at its new accessors and its "indices" at its new index accessor, with its other members as is. Print "bake: mesh " plus the mesh name plus " rebuilt with " plus the vertex count. A changed image whose source is a PNG but whose alpha is 255 at every pixel is written as a JPEG (quality 92, mime "image/jpeg") to keep the file small.
